;;; DRAFT -- set-car!
;;; Edited 2026-08-03 by Priya.
;;; AUTHORED, not model-seeded. The drafting batch
;;; (scratch/pairs/draft_records.py, see draft_run.log) skipped
;;; this verb because an edited draft already existed, so no raw
;;; model output for it survives. Claiming model provenance here
;;; would be provenance nobody can check.
;;; Structure, examples, row programs, caveats and provenance are
;;; authored, not generated.
;;;
;;; Every :note is the literal placeholder PENDING-ENGINE. NOTHING in this
;;; file was run. The notes are filled by a deterministic script once the
;;; cons-cell engine lands. A printed value here would be a value nobody ran.
;;;
;;; Spec basis: ../spec-pack/set-car-bang.slatl -- read ../spec-pack/00-SOURCES.md first,
;;; the normative SRFI-1 text is NOT in this repo.

(verb
  :name "set-car!"
  :std "R7RS-small"
  :library "list"
  :tier "core"
  :kind "state-change"
  :signature "(set-car! pair obj) -> unspecified"
  :summary "Replace the car of a pair in place. The pair keeps its identity, so everything holding it sees the new value."
  :explanation "set-car! is one of the two mutators R7RS-small 6.4 defines on pairs, and it is as small as a verb gets: it overwrites one field of one cell. Nothing is allocated and nothing is relinked. What makes it worth a record is the consequence rather than the operation -- the pair keeps its identity, so every binding that reached that pair, by any route, now reads the new value. A list is a graph of shared cells, and set-car! changes the graph for everyone at once. There is no test for set-car! in the conformance suite (list.sld names it at :162 with no body), so this record rests on R7RS-small 6.4 as cited in spec/R7RS-SMALL-PRIMITIVE-FLOOR.slat:192. Motoi cannot implement it correctly under the flat-array representation, because on a proper list there is no pair object to write into; the existing record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat already carries :status \"needs-alfred\" for exactly this reason, and its examples assert the current wrong behaviour as though it were correct."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice"
     :code "(define p (cons 1 2))\n(set-car! p 99)\n(display p)\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate"
     :code ";; the pair keeps its identity, so every holder sees the change.\n(define p (cons 1 2))\n(define also-p p)\n(set-car! p 99)\n(display (list 'p p 'also-p also-p))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert"
     :code ";; writing into a cell of a list changes the list for everyone.\n(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(define shared xs)\n(set-car! (cdr xs) 'changed)\n(display (list 'xs xs 'shared shared))\n(newline)"
     :note "PENDING-ENGINE")
  )
  :caveats ("The pair must be a pair. Calling it on the empty list, a vector or a number is an error."
             "The return value is unspecified. Do not use the result of set-car! for anything."
             "Every holder of the pair sees the new car immediately. There is no copy and no notification."
             "Literal pairs from quoted data must not be mutated; R7RS-small leaves that undefined and implementations do differ."
             "There is NO test for set-car! in scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld (named at :162, no body)."
             "MOTOI DIVERGES TODAY: a proper list is a flat array with no pair object to write into. scheme/reference/39-list.slat already records this with :status \"needs-alfred\", and its examples currently assert the divergent behaviour as correct -- they must be rewritten when the cons-cell engine lands.")
  :drawbacks ("It is the smallest possible action-at-a-distance: one write, arbitrarily many observers, no way to enumerate them."
             "It makes a value that was safe to share into one that is not, retroactively."
             "Nothing in a function signature can say \"I may set-car! your pairs\".")
  :usecases ("Building mutable records out of pairs, where the car is a field."
             "In-place transformation of a list you own, as map! does internally."
             "Circular and self-referential structures, where the value must be written after the cell exists.")
  :related ("set-cdr!"
             "cons"
             "car"
             "map!"
             "list-copy")
  :learn (:concept "Identity, and why one write has many readers"
          :prerequisites ("cons" "car" "eq?" "list-copy")
          :progression "Bind one pair to two names, write through one, read through the other. That two-line program is the whole concept of mutable shared state.")
  :provenance "R7RS-small 6.4 (cited spec/R7RS-SMALL-PRIMITIVE-FLOOR.slat:192). NAMED at scratch/r7rs-conformance/larceny/list.sld:162 with NO TEST BODY. An existing record in scheme/reference/39-list.slat already carries :status \"needs-alfred\". Drafted 2026-08-03 against the incoming cons-cell representation; NOT RUN."
  :status "needs-alfred"
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define p (cons 1 2))\n(set-car! p 99)\n(display p)\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define p (cons 1 2))\n(set-car! p 99)\n(display (list 'car (car p) 'cdr-unchanged (cdr p)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define p (cons 1 2))\n(define q p)\n(set-car! p 99)\n(display (list 'other-name-sees-it (car q)))\n(newline)"
     :problem "The operator wants to change the first field of a record built as a pair. Candidates: rebuild the pair with cons and rebind (a new object, so other holders keep the old one -- which is either the bug or the whole point), or set-car! (same object, every holder updated). The question is not which is faster. It is whether the other holders should see the change, and that is a design decision the code should state out loud."
     :explanation "eng: the pair now holds the new value. math: only the car changes; the cdr and the identity are untouched. cs: a second name for the pair reads the new value, which is the entire observable content of the verb."
     :meta "Rebuild versus mutate is a question about who should observe the change. Answer it deliberately or the answer gets made by accident.")
    (:eng "Decide who should see the change before deciding how to make it."
     :math "Mutation preserves identity and changes state; reconstruction preserves state and changes identity."
     :cs "The two-name program is the minimal demonstration of shared mutable state, and it belongs in every explanation of it.")
  )
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "Changing what is stored in a place, without changing which place it is."
     :rungs (
             (:domain "list" :code "(define p (cons 'old 'rest))\n(set-car! p 'new)\n(display p)\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the label on a numbered platform" :code "(define platform (cons 'destination-a 'platform-1))\n(set-car! platform 'destination-b)\n(display platform)\n(newline)")
             (:domain "the contents of a numbered pigeonhole" :code "(define hole (cons 'letter 'box-7))\n(set-car! hole 'parcel)\n(display hole)\n(newline)")
     )
     :explanation "In list, the car field is overwritten and the cell is the same cell. Changing the destination shown on a numbered platform does not renumber the platform, and everyone who was told to go to platform 1 now sees a different destination. Replacing the contents of a pigeonhole leaves the pigeonhole where it was. All three share an address that survives a change of contents."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever things are found by address rather than by value.")
    (:eng "Address stability is what makes the update visible to everyone who was given the address."
     :math "A mutable cell is a function from time to values, indexed by a stable identity."
     :cs "This is the difference between a variable and a value, and it is the foundation of every data structure that changes.")
  )
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(define p (cons 1 2))\n(set-car! p 99)\n(display (car p))\n(newline)"
     :math-program "(define p (cons 1 2))\n(define d (cdr p))\n(set-car! p 99)\n(display (list 'cdr-untouched (eq? (cdr p) d)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-program "(define xs (list 1 2 3))\n(define alias xs)\n(set-car! xs 99)\n(display (list 'alias-changed (car alias)))\n(newline)"
     :claim "set-car! replaces the car field of the given pair with obj. The pair's identity and its cdr are unchanged, so (eq? p p) still holds and every other reference to p reads the new car. The return value is unspecified."
     :invariant "forall pair p, object o. after set-car!(p, o): eq?(car(p), o) and cdr(p) is unchanged by identity and p is unchanged by identity"
     :meta "The pattern is field assignment on a shared object. What would falsify the claim: a changed cdr, a new pair returned in place of mutation, or an alias that does not see the write -- and that last case is exactly what the flat-array representation produces on a proper list, which is why this record and the one in 39-list.slat both carry :status needs-alfred.")
    (:eng "The alias program is the one that fails on the current engine. Keep it at the front of the proof."
     :math "Changing one field must leave the other alone; that is a separate assertion and it is worth making."
     :cs "An unspecified return value means the verb is called for effect, and code that reads its result is wrong even where it happens to work.")
  )
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (make-cell v) (cons v '()))\n(define (cell-set! c v) (set-car! c v))\n(define c (make-cell 1))\n(cell-set! c 2)\n(display (car c))\n(newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (swap! p q)\n  (let ((tmp (car p)))\n    (set-car! p (car q))\n    (set-car! q tmp)))\n(define a (cons 1 'x))\n(define b (cons 2 'y))\n(swap! a b)\n(display (list (car a) (car b)))\n(newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (map-in-place! f xs)\n  (pair-for-each (lambda (p) (set-car! p (f (car p)))) xs)\n  xs)\n(display (map-in-place! (lambda (n) (* n n)) (list 1 2 3)))\n(newline)"
     :base "set-car! writes a new value into the first field of an existing pair."
     :emergent "eng: a one-slot mutable box, which is the smallest useful stateful object and the basis of every larger one. math: swapping two cars needs a temporary, and the reason is the same reason it always is -- the first write destroys the value the second write needs. cs: map! written out of set-car! and pair-for-each, which is how it is actually implemented and the version worth reading before using the built-in."
     :meta "The family is mutable state built from one primitive. What emerges is that boxes, records, in-place maps and object systems are all this verb with more names on top, so understanding its aliasing behaviour is not optional.")
    (:eng "Everything stateful in the language is built from this. Learn its aliasing before you build on it."
     :math "The temporary in a swap is not ceremony; it is what the sequencing of destructive writes requires."
     :cs "Reading the four-line implementation of map! teaches more than the verb's documentation does.")
  )
)
